//- 0:00 TITLE, then 0:15 COLOR DOESN'T EXIST: the thesis, said plainly. Straight in: no hello, no who-am-I. Everything after this is evidence for it. docs/TALK.md has the run.

section.cover(data-timing="15")
  p.a-talk-by a talk by #{authorHandle}
  h1= talkTitle
  aside.notes
    ul
      li Straight in. Every second before 4:00 counts toward the vote.

//- The deck's one shout on the yes path. This is the line that earns it.
section.statement.is-shout(data-timing="15")
  h2 Color doesn't exist.
  p Light has a wavelength. That's all it has.
  aside.notes
    ul
      li Say it flat. No hedging.
      li Visible light is a sliver, about 380 to 700 nanometres, between infrared and ultraviolet.

section.statement(data-timing="20")
  h2 Your eye writes down three numbers.
  p.aside (every screen in this room is lying to you)
  aside.notes
    ul
      li Three kinds of cone squash a whole spectrum into three numbers. Say cones, not rods and cones: rods don't see color.
      li Different spectra with the same three numbers look identical. That's how RGB screens work.
      li The screens aside is the first thing to cut if the run is long.

section.statement(data-timing="25")
  h2 Your brain makes up the rest.
  p Every color you've ever named was your head pretending to be the world.
  aside.notes
    ul
      li The thesis. Everything after this is evidence.
      li Draft line: say it in your own words. Naming a color passes off something that happens in your head as something out in the world.
